by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-02T07:55:44Z

Thanks Amoifr, the two-value option is exactly right. Rebased on 8.2 and made three changes before merging.

My earlier advice on the resource message was half wrong, sorry. Reverting it did not fix the failure, it moved it: Fabbot lints every file a PR touches, and it wants the quoted form. So the message is now `'"%s" resource'` and the assertion in `XmlEncoderTest` was updated to match. That clears the job.

`null` in a per-call context now really disables the option. `getBooleanRepr()` read it with `??`, so `null` fell through to the constructor default and `withBooleanRepr(null)` was a no-op against a configured encoder, despite the `?array` signature. It reads the key with `array_key_exists()` now, with a test.

Empty strings are rejected. `['', 'no']` made true and false indistinguishable, and combined with `IGNORE_EMPTY_ATTRIBUTES` it silently dropped every attribute whose value was `true`. Two provider cases cover it.

On making `['', '']` mean the XHTML idiom, true written as `foo="foo"` and false omitted: it reads well for attributes, but this option also governs element text, where `true` would have to become `<foo>foo</foo>` and `false` would have to remove the element, which is what `REMOVE_EMPTY_TAGS` already decides. It also changes which nodes exist rather than how a value is spelled. Rejecting empty strings keeps that pair free, so it can get its own explicit spelling later if we want it.

Left alone, worth knowing: the option is validated on each boolean node rather than once, and the boolean branch skips CDATA wrapping, so `['<yes>', '<no>']` gives `&lt;yes&gt;` where the same literal string would be wrapped in CDATA. Both harmless.
